PART 2: THE OLD APPROACH - TRADITIONAL WEB DEVELOPMENT

================================================================================

How Traditional Websites Work

Traditionally, websites are built using three main technologies:

Technology | Purpose                       | Examples
-----------|-------------------------------|-------------------------------
HTML       | Structure and content         | Headings, paragraphs, buttons
CSS        | Styling and layout            | Colors, fonts, spacing
JavaScript | Interactivity                 | Click handlers, animations


In the traditional approach described here, the server stores and serves the application resources and can render the page before sending it to the browser.

================================================================================

THE CLIENT-SERVER ARCHITECTURE

================================================================================

The old approach has two main sides:

Part             | Responsibility
-----------------|------------------------------------------------
Server/Back End  | Processes requests
                 | Can render the complete HTML page
                 | Sends pages/resources to the client
-----------------|------------------------------------------------
Client/Front End | User-facing side of the application
                 | Runs in the browser
                 | Sends requests and displays responses
-----------------|------------------------------------------------
Browser          | Runs on the user's computer/device
                 | Displays HTML/CSS and executes JavaScript
                 | Communicates with the server


================================================================================

HOW A REQUEST WORKS IN THE OLD APPROACH

================================================================================

Step-by-step flow:

Step | Action
-----|------------------------------------------------
1    | User opens browser and visits website
2    | Browser sends request to server
3    | Server receives request
4    | Server renders the HTML page
5    | Server sends the complete page to browser
6    | Browser displays the page
7    | User sees the website


Easy flow:

USER
  ↓
BROWSER
  ↓
REQUEST
  ↓
SERVER
  ↓
RENDER PAGE
  ↓
FULL HTML PAGE
  ↓
BROWSER DISPLAYS PAGE


================================================================================

WHAT HAPPENS WHEN THE USER INTERACTS?

================================================================================

Example: User clicks a button that requires a server round trip.

Step | Action
-----|----------------------------------------------------
1    | User clicks button
2    | Browser sends request to server
3    | Server processes request
4    | Server renders the page again
5    | Server sends the new page to browser
6    | Browser displays the new page
7    | User sees updated website

Main Problem
Small Change
   ↓
Server Request
   ↓
Entire Page Re-rendered
   ↓
Complete Page Returned
   ↓
Page Reload


Even when only a small part changes, this traditional interaction can result in the entire page being returned and reloaded.

================================================================================

PROBLEMS WITH THE OLD APPROACH

================================================================================

Problem 1: Full Page Reload

Every interaction that requires a new server-rendered page may involve:

Complete round trip to the server
Re-rendering the HTML page
Downloading the new page/resources as required
Browser navigation/page refresh
Result
--------------------------------
Slower interaction
Noticeable delay
Reduced user experience

Problem 2: Inefficiency

Example:

Counter = 5
    ↓
User clicks Increment
    ↓
Counter should become 6
    ↓
Server renders page
    ↓
Page is returned
    ↓
Browser reloads it


Only the counter changed, but the traditional page navigation may reload much more than that small value.

Result
--------------------------------
More network usage
More server-side processing
More browser work

Problem 3: Visual Flashing

During a traditional page reload:

Old Page
   ↓
Navigation / Reload
   ↓
New Page Loaded
   ↓
New Content Displayed


A visible blank/loading transition may occur, sometimes experienced as a white flash.

Result
--------------------------------
Less seamless interaction
User notices page reload
Application can feel slower

Problem 4: Poor User Experience

Users may have to wait for:

Server Request
     ↓
Server Processing
     ↓
Page Rendering
     ↓
Network Transfer
     ↓
Browser Rendering

Result
--------------------------------
More waiting
Possible user frustration
Less responsive experience
Potential business impact


