PART 5: HOW REACT FITS IN

================================================================================

What is React?

React is a JavaScript library used for building user interfaces and is commonly used to build SPAs.

React helps developers:

Capability             | Purpose
-----------------------|---------------------------------------------
State Management       | Manage changing application data
UI Updates             | Update UI when state changes
Components             | Build reusable UI pieces
Event Handling         | Respond to user interactions
Complex Interfaces     | Organize larger interactive applications


================================================================================

REACT'S ROLE

================================================================================

USER INTERACTION
       ↓
EVENT HANDLER
       ↓
STATE CHANGES
       ↓
REACT RE-RENDERS
       ↓
NECESSARY UI UPDATE
       ↓
BROWSER DISPLAYS CHANGE


================================================================================

WHY REACT IS USEFUL

================================================================================

Without React - Vanilla JavaScript
let count = 5;

const counterDisplay =
  document.getElementById('counter');

const incrementBtn =
  document.getElementById('incrementBtn');

incrementBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {

  count = count + 1;

  counterDisplay.textContent =
    `Counter: ${count}`;
});


With direct DOM manipulation, developers manually identify and update elements.

Concern                    | Vanilla JavaScript Example
---------------------------|----------------------------------------
Element Selection          | document.getElementById(...)
Event Handling             | addEventListener(...)
DOM Update                 | textContent = ...
State                      | Managed manually in variables/logic
Larger Applications        | DOM/state synchronization gets harder

With React
import React, { useState } from 'react';

export default function Counter() {

  const [count, setCount] = useState(5);

  return (
    <div>

      <h1>
        Counter: {count}
      </h1>

      <button
        onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}
      >
        Increment Counter
      </button>

    </div>
  );
}

Advantages
React Feature        | Benefit
---------------------|------------------------------------------
Declarative UI       | Describe the UI for the current state
State                | useState manages changing data
Automatic UI Update  | State change causes re-render
Components           | UI can be divided into reusable pieces
Readable Structure   | UI and interaction logic are organized


Easy comparison:

VANILLA JAVASCRIPT

Find DOM Element
      ↓
Listen for Event
      ↓
Change Data
      ↓
Manually Update DOM


REACT

User Event
      ↓
Update State
      ↓
React Re-renders
      ↓
UI Reflects State


