PART 9: KEY CONCEPTS SUMMARY

================================================================================

Concept 1: Server-Side Rendering

Server-side rendering means HTML is generated on the server and sent to the browser.

SERVER
   ↓
RENDER HTML
   ↓
SEND HTML
   ↓
BROWSER
   ↓
DISPLAY PAGE

Aspect      | Explanation
------------|----------------------------------------
Rendering   | HTML generated on server
Browser     | Receives rendered HTML
Useful For  | Content pages, SEO-related scenarios
Trade-off   | Navigation may involve server requests


================================================================================

Concept 2: Client-Side Rendering

================================================================================

Client-side rendering uses JavaScript in the browser to generate/update the UI.

SERVER
   ↓
APPLICATION / DATA
   ↓
BROWSER + JAVASCRIPT
   ↓
RENDER / UPDATE UI

Aspect      | Explanation
------------|---------------------------------------------
Rendering   | Much of UI rendering occurs in browser
Server      | Often exposes APIs/data
JavaScript  | Controls dynamic UI
Useful For  | Interactive applications
Advantage   | Smooth client-side updates


================================================================================

Concept 3: Single Page Application - SPA

================================================================================

SPA
↓
Initial application/page loaded
↓
JavaScript handles interactions
↓
Necessary UI portions change
↓
Full page navigation avoided for many interactions


Examples commonly associated with SPA-style experiences:

Gmail
Google Maps
Facebook
Netflix


================================================================================

Concept 4: AJAX

================================================================================

AJAX = Asynchronous JavaScript and XML

AJAX allows a browser to communicate with a server asynchronously without requiring a full page reload for the interaction.

BROWSER
   ↓
ASYNCHRONOUS REQUEST
   ↓
SERVER / API
   ↓
DATA RESPONSE
   ↓
JAVASCRIPT
   ↓
UPDATE PAGE


Modern applications commonly use APIs such as fetch() for asynchronous HTTP requests.

Example:

fetch('/api/data')


The important concept is:

REQUEST DATA
     ↓
DON'T RELOAD WHOLE PAGE
     ↓
PROCESS RESPONSE
     ↓
UPDATE REQUIRED UI


